{
  "name": "Austerlitz",
  "campaign": "austerlitz-1805",
  "summary": "2 December 1805, and the only Field on the shelf that is a piece of a battle rather than a battle. Austerlitz is ten kilometres wide; this is the three in the middle that decided the other seven. Napoleon has given the Pratzen plateau away on purpose and left his right at Sokolnitz visibly thin, because the allied plan he wants them to have is to march round that right — and the only way round it is to come down off the high ground in the centre and cross his front to get there. They are doing it when the clock starts. What is left on the crest is one column strung out in the order it was walking in, and what is in the bottom of the Goldbach, out of sight of all of them, is two divisions with orders to go straight up. The Field is a reading of that ground rather than a survey of it, at about half scale: eighty metres of climb, no Crossing anywhere, and a crest with nothing on it to hide behind, because what hides a battalion here is the reverse slope and nothing else. Telnitz and the frozen ponds are off the south edge, which is where the allied left goes if it loses.",
